<section xml:id="common-transforms">
	<title>Common Transforms</title>
	
	<introduction>
		<p>
			Now that we've defined the Laplace transform, the next step is to build a basic toolkit of common transforms. These are formulas for frequently encountered functions, such as constants, exponentials, powers of <m>t</m>, and trigonometric functions. Learning these transforms will allow you to compute Laplace transforms quickly, without having to evaluate integrals from scratch each time.
		</p>

		<p>
			We'll derive these formulas using straightforward examples and then generalize the results. You'll see how the Laplace transform simplifies for each type of function and how certain patterns repeat across cases.
		</p>
	</introduction>

	<subsection label="constant-function-transform">
		<title>Laplace Transform of a Constant</title>

		<exercise label="lt-common-transforms-prereading-questions-bundle">
			<title>🤔💭 Exponential Functions Pre-Reading Questions</title>
			<task label="lt-common-transforms-prereading-questions-1"><title>📖❓ Exponential Integration Rules</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Match each exponential integrand with its antiderivative. Assume <m>K</m> is a constant.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<cardsort>
					<match>
						<premise><m>\ds \int e^{5t} dt</m></premise>
						<response><m>\ds \frac{1}{5} e^{5t} + C</m></response>
					</match>
					<match>
						<premise><m>\ds \int e^{-3t} dt</m></premise>
						<response><m>\ds \frac{-1}{3} e^{-3t} + C</m></response>
					</match>
					<match>
						<premise><m>\ds \int e^{Kt} dt</m></premise>
						<response><m>\ds \frac{1}{K} e^{Kt} + C</m></response>
					</match>
					<match>
						<premise><m>\ds \int e^{(7-s)t} dt</m></premise>
						<response><m>\ds \frac{1}{7 - s} e^{(7-s)t} + C</m></response>
					</match>
				</cardsort>
			</task>
			<task label="lt-common-transforms-prereading-questions-2"><title>📖❓ How is <m>s</m> Treated During Integration?</title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						In the Laplace transform integral, the variable <m>s</m> is treated as a <fillin characters="10" /> during the integration process.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p>constant</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								Correct! When evaluating the integral, <m>s</m> is treated as a constant, since the integration is with respect to <m>t</m>.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>variable</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								No, even though <m>s</m> is a variable overall, it is treated as a constant during the integration.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>coefficient</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								No, while <m>s</m> acts like a coefficient in <m>e^{-st}</m>, it's conceptually treated as a constant in the integration.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>limit</p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>
								No, <m>s</m> is not a limit of integration; it appears inside the integrand.
							</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>
			<task label="lt-common-transforms-prereading-questions-3"><title>📖❓ Choosing <m>u</m> and <m>dv</m> in <m>\int t^2 e^{-st} dt</m></title>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Which functions should you choose as <m>u</m> and <m>dv</m> when applying integration by parts to <m>\int t^2 e^{-st} dt</m>?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><m>u = t^2,\quad dv = e^{-st} dt</m></p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>Correct! This choice simplifies with each integration by parts.</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><m>u = e^{-st},\quad dv = t^2 dt</m></p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>No, this makes integration more difficult. We want to differentiate <m>t^2</m> and integrate the exponential.</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><m>u = st,\quad dv = t dt</m></p></statement>
						<feedback>
							<p>These choices are not aligned with the integrand <m>t^2 e^{-st}</m>.</p>
						</feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>
		</exercise>

		<p>
			<idx><h>Laplace transform of a constant</h></idx>
			We begin with the simplest case: the constant function <m>f(t) = 1</m>. By the definition of the <xref ref="gi-laplace-transform" text="title"/>,
			<md>
				\lap{1} = \int_0^\infty e^{-st}\, dt.
			</md>
		</p>

		<p>
			This integral converges when <m>s \gt 0</m> and leads to our first Laplace transform rule:
		</p>

		<assemblage xml:id="lap-L1">
			<title>📜 Laplace Transform of <m>1</m></title>
			<p>
				<md>
					<mrow xml:id="lt-L1">
						\text{L}_1:\quad\lap{1} = \frac{1}{s}, \quad s \gt 0.
					</mrow>
				</md>
				<xref	ref="lt-L1-derivation-details" text="title"/>
			</p>
		</assemblage>

		<p>
			Although this transform is specific to the constant function <m>1</m>, we will see later that it will serve as the transform formula for all constant functions. 
		</p>
	</subsection>

	<subsection label="exponential-function-transform">
		<title>Laplace Transform of Exponentials</title>

		<p>
			<idx><h>Laplace transform of an exponential</h></idx>
			To get the transform formula for exponentials of the form <m>f(t) = e^{at}</m>, where <m>a</m> is a constant, we will first find it for <m>e^{7t}</m>:
		</p>

		<example label="lt-example-2">
			<title>Laplace Transform of <m>e^{7t}</m></title>
			<statement>
				<p><md>\lap{e^{7t}} = \frac{1}{s - 7}, \quad s \gt 7</md></p>
			</statement>
			<solution xml:id="common-exp-7t-details">
				<p>
					Using the definition of the Laplace transform, we get
					<md>
						\lap{ e^{7t} }
						= \int_0^{\infty} e^{-st} \cdot e^{7t}\ dt
						= \lim_{b \to \infty}\int_0^b e^{(7-s)t}\ dt
					</md>.
				</p>

				<p>
					Focusing just on the integral, we let <m>u=(7-s)t</m> and apply <m>u</m>-substitution:
					<md>
						<mrow>
							\int_0^b e^{(7-s)t}\, dt = \frac{1}{7-s} e^{(7-s)t} \Bigg|_0^b
							\amp = \frac{1}{7-s} \left( e^{(7-s)b} - e^{0} \right)
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \frac{1}{7-s} \left( e^{(7-s)b} - 1 \right)
						</mrow>
					</md>
					<xref ref="lt-example-2-details" text="title"/>.
				</p>

				<p>
					Now, we compute the limit of this expression as <m>b \to \infty</m>:
					<md>
						<mrow>
							\lim_{b \to \infty} \frac{1}{7-s} \left( e^{(7-s)b} - 1 \right)
							\amp = \frac{1}{7-s} \Bigg[ \ub{\lim_{b \to \infty} e^{(7-s)b}}_{L} - \lim_{b \to \infty} 1 \Bigg]
						</mrow>
					</md>.
				</p>

				<p>
					As before, <m>L</m> goes to <m>0</m> if the <term><xref ref="exponential-end-behavior" text="custom">power of the exponential function</xref></term> goes to <m>-\infty</m>. This only happens if <m>7-s</m> is negative. That is, <m>\ 7-s \lt 0\ </m> or <m>\ 7 \lt s</m>. 
				</p>

				<p>
					So, as long as <m>s \gt 7</m>, then <m>L \to 0</m> and the transform becomes 
					<md>
						\lap{e^{7t}} = \frac{1}{s - 7}, \quad s \gt 7
					</md>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>

		<p>
			Replacing <m>7</m> with an <m>a</m> in this solution leads to the general formula:
		</p>

		<p>
			For any constant <m>a</m>, the Laplace transform of <m>e^{at}</m> is given by:
		</p>

		<assemblage xml:id="lap-L2">
			<title>📜 Laplace Transform of <m>e^{at}</m></title>
			<p>
				<md>
					<mrow xml:id="lt-L2">
						\text{L}_2:\quad\lap{e^{at}} = \frac{1}{s - a}, \quad s \gt a.
					</mrow>
				</md>
				<xref ref="lt-L2-derivation-details" text="title"/>.
			</p>
		</assemblage>

		<exercise label="exponential-function-transform-chkpt-1">
			<title>📖❓  Give the Transform</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<md>\lap{e^{-3t}} = <fillin characters="2"/> </md>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p><m>\ds\frac{1}{s+3}</m></p></statement>
					<feedback>
						<p>
							Correct! The Laplace transform of <m>e^{-3t}</m> is <m>\ds\frac{1}{s+3}</m>.
						</p>
					</feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><m>\ds\frac{1}{s-3}</m></p></statement>
					<feedback>
						<p>
							No, this would be the transform of <m>e^{3t}</m>. Double-check the sign of the exponent.
						</p>
					</feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><m>\ds\frac{1}{s-3t}</m></p></statement>
					<feedback>
						<p>
							No, <m>t</m> should not appear in the final expression, only <m>s</m>.
						</p>
					</feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><m>\ds\frac{3}{s+3}</m></p></statement>
					<feedback>
						<p>
							No, the numerator should be 1, not 3. This is not a scaled exponential.
						</p>
					</feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>
	</subsection>

	<subsection label="power-function-transform">
		<title>Laplace Transform of Powers of <m>t</m></title>

		<p>
			<idx><h>Laplace transform of a power function</h></idx>
			Now let's consider power functions, <m>f(t) = t^n</m> where <m>n</m> is a non-negative integer.
			<md>
				\lap{t^n} = \frac{n!}{s^{n+1}}, \quad s > 0.
			</md>
		</p>

		<p>
			We already found the transforms for <m>n = 0</m> and <m>n = 1</m> since
			<ul>
				<li><m>n=0:\qquad\lap{t^0} = \lap{1} = \dfrac{1}{s}</m></li>
				<li><m>n=1:\qquad\lap{t^1} = \lap{t} = \dfrac{1}{s^2}</m></li>
			</ul>
			We could find <m>\lap{t^2}</m> next, but it is more instructive to show the following relationship:
		</p>

		<example label="lt-example-tsqrd">
			<title>Laplace Transform of <m>t^2</m></title>

			<statement>
				<p>
					<md>\lap{t^2} = \frac{2}{s}\cdot \lap{t}, \quad s \gt 0</md>
				</p>
			</statement>

			<solution label="common-t-sqrd-details">
				<p>
					Applying the definition and writing the integral as a limit, we have
					<md>
						\lap{ t^2 } = \lim_{b \to \infty} \ub{\int_0^b e^{-st} \cdot t^2\ dt}_{I}
					</md>.
				</p>

				<p>
					Applying integration by parts to <m>I</m> shows:
					<md>
						<mrow>
							\lap{ t^2 }
							\amp = \lim_{b \to \infty} \left[-\frac{b^2}{s}e^{-sb} + \frac{2}{s}\int_0^b e^{-st}\cdot t\ dt\right]
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = -\frac{1}{s} \lim_{b \to \infty} \left[-\frac{b^2}{e^{sb}}\right] + \frac{2}{s}\lim_{b \to \infty}\int_0^b e^{-st}\cdot t\ dt
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = -\frac{1}{s}\ub{\lim_{b \to \infty}\frac{b^2}{e^{sb}}}_{L} + \frac{2}{s}\ub{\int_0^\infty e^{-st}\cdot t\ dt}_{\large\lap{t}}.
						</mrow>
					</md>
					<xref ref="lt-power-function-transform-ibp-1-details" text="title"/>.
				</p>

				<p>
					Noticing that the improper integral is just <m>\lap{t}</m>, we have
					<md>
						\lap{ t^2 } = -\frac{1}{s}\cdot L + \frac{2}{s}\cdot \lap{t}
					</md>,
					but <m>L=0</m> if <m>s \gt 0</m>, so we have the relationship
					<md>
						\lap{t^2} = \frac{2}{s}\cdot \lap{t}, \quad s \gt 0
					</md>
					<xref ref="lt-example-tsqrd-LH-details" text="title"/>.
				</p>
			</solution>	
		</example>
		
		<p>
			You can use this work as a template for a general recursive relationship:
		</p>

		<example label="lt-example-tnth">
			<title>Laplace Transform of <m>t^n</m></title>

			<statement>
				<p><md>\lap{t^n} = \frac{n}{s}\cdot \lap{t^{n-1}}, \quad s \gt 0</md></p>
			</statement>
			<solution label="common-recursive-power-function-details">
				<p>
					Replacing <m>2</m> with <m>n</m> in the previous solution, we have
					<md>
						\lap{ t^n } = \lim_{b \to \infty} \ub{\int_0^b e^{-st} \cdot t^n\ dt}_{I}
					</md>.
				</p>

				<p>
					Apply integration by parts to <m>I</m>:
					<md>
						<mrow>
							\lap{ t^{n} }
							\amp = \lim_{b \to \infty} \left[-\frac{b^n}{e^{sb}} + \frac{n}{s}\int_0^b e^{-st}\cdot t^{n-1}\ dt\right]
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = -\frac{1}{s} \lim_{b \to \infty} \left[-\frac{b^n}{e^{sb}}\right] + \frac{n}{s}\lim_{b \to \infty}\int_0^b e^{-st}\cdot t^{n-1}\ dt
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = -\frac{1}{s}\ub{\lim_{b \to \infty}\frac{b^n}{e^{sb}}}_{L} + \frac{n}{s}\ub{\int_0^\infty e^{-st}\cdot t^{n-1}\ dt}_{\large\lap{t^{n-1}}}.
						</mrow>
					</md>
					<xref ref="lt-example-tnth-IVP-details" text="title"/>.
				</p>

				<p>
					Noticing that the improper integral is just <m>\lap{t^{n-1}}</m>, we have
					<md>
						\lap{ t^{n} } = -\frac{1}{s}\cdot L + \frac{n}{s}\cdot \lap{t^{n-1}}
					</md>.
				</p>

				<p>
					Since <m>L=0</m> if <m>s \gt 0</m>, we have
					<md>
						\lap{t^{n}} = \frac{n}{s}\cdot \lap{t^{n-1}}, \quad s \gt 0
					</md>
					<xref ref="lt-example-tnth-LH-details" text="title"/>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>

		<p>
			To get the general formula, we apply this relationship repeatedly for increasing <m>n</m>:
			<md> 
				\begin{array}{crcl}
					n=1:	\amp \DLO\lap{t} \amp =	\amp 
						\dfrac{1}{s}\cdot\lap{1} = \dfrac{1}{s}\cdot\dfrac{1}{s} = {\DLO\dfrac{1}{s^2}}\\
					n=2:	\amp \DLBa\lap{t^2}	\amp =	\amp 
						\dfrac{2}{s}\cdot{\DLO\lap{t}} = \dfrac{2}{s}\cdot{\DLO\dfrac{1}{s^2}} = {\DLBa\dfrac{2\cdot 1}{s^3}}\\
					n=3:	\amp \DLGb\lap{t^3}	\amp =	\amp 
						\dfrac{3}{s}\cdot{\DLBa\lap{t^2}} = \dfrac{3}{s}\cdot{\DLBa\dfrac{2\cdot 1}{s^3}} = {\DLGb\dfrac{3\cdot 2\cdot 1}{s^4}}\\
					n=4:	\amp \DLRa\lap{t^4}	\amp =	\amp
						\dfrac{4}{s}\cdot{\DLGb\lap{t^3}} = \dfrac{4}{s}\cdot{\DLGb\dfrac{3\cdot 2\cdot 1}{s^4}} = {\DLRa\dfrac{4\cdot 3\cdot 2\cdot 1}{s^5}}\\
					\vdots	\amp \vdots\quad\amp \amp \qquad\qquad\vdots\\
				\end{array}
			</md>
		</p>

		<p>
			This pattern suggests that, in general, we have
			<md>\lap{t^n} =\dfrac{n}{s}\cdot\lap{t^{n-1}} = \dfrac{n}{s}\cdot\dfrac{(n-1)\dotsm 4\cdot 3\cdot 2\cdot 1}{s^{n}},</md>
			which can be summarized as follows:
		</p>

		<assemblage xml:id="lap-L3">
			<title>📜 Laplace Transform of <m>t^n</m></title>
			<p>
				<md>
					<mrow xml:id="lt-L3">
						\text{L}_3:\quad\lap{t^n} = \frac{n!}{s^{n+1}}, \quad s \gt 0, \quad n = 0, 1, 2, \ldots.
					</mrow>
				</md>
				<xref ref="lt-L3-derivation-details" text="title"/>.
			</p>
		</assemblage>
	</subsection>
	
	<subsection label="sine-cosine-function-transform">
		<title>Laplace Transforms of Sine and Cosine</title>

		<p>
			<idx><h>Laplace transform of sine</h></idx>
			<idx><h>Laplace transform of cosine</h></idx>
			Finally, we derive the Laplace transforms of <m>\sin(bt)</m> and <m>\cos(bt)</m>.
		</p>

		<p>
			To make the derivation easier, we will find the transform of <m>\cos(3t)</m> first.
		</p>

		<example label="lt-example-cos-3t">
			<title>Laplace Transform of <m>\cos(3t)</m></title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<md>\lap{\cos(3t)} = \frac{s}{s^2 + 3^2}, \quad s \gt 0</md>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Applying the definition and writing the integral as a limit, we have
					<md>
						\lap{ \cos(3t)} = \int_0^{\infty} e^{-st} \cdot \cos(3t)\ dt = \lim_{b \to \infty} \ub{\int_0^{b} e^{-st} \cos(3t)\ dt}_{I}
					</md>.
				</p>

				<p>
					Treating <m>s</m> as constant, we apply integration by parts to <m>I</m> as follows:
					<md>
						I = -\frac{e^{-st}}{s} \cos(3t) \Bigg|_0^b - \frac{3}{s}\int_0^{b} e^{-st} \sin(3t)\ dt
					</md>,
				</p>

				<aside><title>Integration by Parts</title>
					<p>
						1st Integration by Parts
						<md>
							<mrow>	u	\amp = \cos(3t)				</mrow>
							<mrow>	du	\amp = -3\sin(3t)\ dt		</mrow>
							<mrow>	v	\amp = -\sfrac{e^{-st}}{s}	</mrow>
							<mrow>	dv	\amp = e^{-st}\ dt			</mrow>
						</md>
						2nd Integration by Parts
						<md>
							<mrow>	u	\amp = \sin(3t)				</mrow>
							<mrow>	du	\amp = 3\cos(3t)\ dt		</mrow>
							<mrow>	v	\amp = -\sfrac{e^{-st}}{s}	</mrow>
							<mrow>	dv	\amp = e^{-st}\ dt			</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</aside>

				<p>
					but this requires a second integration by parts:
					<md>
						<mrow>
							I
							\amp = -\frac{e^{-st}}{s} \cos(3t) \Bigg|_0^b - \frac{3}{s} \left[ -\frac{e^{-st}}{s} \sin(3t)\Bigg|_0^b + \frac{3}{s} \int_0^{b} e^{-st} \cos(3t)\ dt \right]
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\amp = \left(-\frac{e^{-st}}{s} \cos(3t) + \frac{3e^{-st}}{s^2} \sin(3t)\right)\Bigg|_0^b - \frac{9}{s^2} \ub{\int_0^{b} e^{-st} \cos(3t)\ dt}_{I}
						</mrow>
					</md>.
				</p>

				<p>
					Since the integral <m>I</m> reappeared, we actually have
					<md>
						I = \left(-\frac{e^{-st}}{s} \cos(3t) + \frac{3e^{-st}}{s^2} \sin(3t)\right)\Bigg|_0^b - \frac{9}{s^2} I
					</md>.
					Evaluating the limits of integration and solving for <m>I</m>, we find
					<md>
						<mrow>
							I + \frac{9}{s^2} I  \amp = \left(-\frac{e^{-st}}{s} \cos(3t) + \frac{3e^{-st}}{s^2} \sin(3t)\right)\Bigg|_0^b
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							I\left(\frac{s^2+9}{s^2}\right) \amp = \left(-\frac{e^{-sb}}{s} \cos(3b) + \frac{3e^{-sb}}{s^2} \sin(3b) + \frac{1}{s} \right)
						</mrow>
						<mrow> 
							I	\amp = \frac{s^2}{s^2+9}\left(-\frac{e^{-sb}}{s} \cos(3b) + \frac{3e^{-sb}}{s^2} \sin(3b) + \frac{1}{s} \right)
						</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>
					Finally, the limit of <m>I</m> as <m>b \to \infty</m> is the sum of the limits
					<md>
						<mrow>
							\lim_{b \to \infty} I = \amp
						</mrow>
						<mrow>
							\frac{s^2}{s^2+9} \amp \Bigg(
								- \frac{1}{s}\ub{\lim_{b \to \infty} e^{-sb} \cos(3b)}_{L_1}
								+ \frac{3}{s^2}\ub{\lim_{b \to \infty} e^{-sb} \sin(3b)}_{L_2} 
								+ \frac{1}{s}
							\Bigg)
						</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>

				<p>
					Since <m>L_1 \to 0</m> &amp; <m>L_2 \to 0</m> if <m>s \gt 0</m>, we can conclude that
					<md>
						\lap{\cos(3t)} = \frac{s}{s^2 + 9}, \quad s \gt 0
					</md>
					<xref ref="lt-example-4-details" text="title"/>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>

		<p>
			This computation, along with a similar one for sine, leads to the following general formulas:
		</p>

		<assemblage xml:id="lap-L4-L5">
			<title>📜 Laplace Transforms of Sine and Cosine</title>
			<p>
				<md>
					<mrow xml:id="lt-L4">
						\text{L}_4:\quad\lap{\sin(bt)} = \frac{b}{s^2 + b^2}, \quad s \gt 0.
					</mrow>
				</md>
				<md>
					<mrow xml:id="lt-L5">
						\text{L}_5:\quad\lap{\cos(bt)} = \frac{s}{s^2 + b^2}, \quad s \gt 0,
					</mrow>
				</md>
				<xref ref="lt-L4-derivation-details" text="title"/>.
			</p>
		</assemblage>
	</subsection>
</section>